<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duan Xianbei</h1><p class="st-meta">250 CE – 357 CE (approx.) · capital Lingzhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Xianbei tribal state in the Liaoxi region that allied with the Jin against the Xiongnu and was ground down by Later Zhao and the Murong.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Duan traced their rise to Riluzhuan, a man sold as a slave to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wuhuan">Wuhuan</a> who gathered a band of followers in the Liaoxi region. By the late 3rd century his descendants led a powerful <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei confederation</a> based at Lingzhi, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianan">Qian'an</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, on the north-eastern frontier of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Duan were cavalry herders under hereditary chiefs who married into the family of the Jin governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jun-pengzu">Wang Jun</a> and fought for him against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a>. Their power depended on alliances and plunder. In 318 the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-pidi">Duan Pidi</a> killed the loyalist Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-kun">Liu Kun</a>, an act widely condemned.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, crushed the Duan at Lingzhi in 338. A remnant under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-kan">Duan Kan</a> ruled the area around Guanggu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> conquered it in 356 to 357, after which the Duan merged with other <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> groups.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 250 CE – 357 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:64.68%;width:2.13%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 CE – 357 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lingzhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duan Pidi, Duan Liao, Duan Kan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Steppe shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duan tribe, Duan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=304&amp;focus=duan-xianbei" aria-label="Duan Xianbei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7349 891 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duan Xianbei lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7669 1147L7855 1179M7669 1147L7867 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7867 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7669 1147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7669 1147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lingzhi. <a href="/atlas?year=304&amp;focus=duan-xianbei">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
